1
00:03:10,714 --> 00:03:16,714
Two dads, one toy, no prisoners.

2
00:06:21,428 --> 00:06:27,428
Jingle All the Way is a family, comedy, adventure film directed by Brian Levant.

3
00:09:32,142 --> 00:09:38,142
Howard Langston, a salesman for a mattress company, is
constantly kept busy at his job, disappointing his son.

4
00:12:42,857 --> 00:12:48,857
After he misses his son's karate exposition, Howard vows to make it up to him by buying
an action figure of his son's favorite television hero for Christmas.

5
00:15:53,571 --> 00:15:59,571
Unfortunately for Howard, it is Christmas Eve, and
every store is sold out of Turbo Man.

6
00:19:04,285 --> 00:19:10,285
Now, Howard must travel all over town and compete with
everybody else to find a Turbo Man action figure.

7
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:21,000
Howard Langston, played by Arnold Schwarzenegger.

8
00:25:25,714 --> 00:25:31,714
Myron Larabee, played by Sinbad.

9
00:28:36,428 --> 00:28:42,428
Ted Maltin, played by Phil Hartman.

10
00:31:47,142 --> 00:31:53,142
Liz Langston, played by Rita Wilson.

11
00:34:57,857 --> 00:35:03,857
Officer Hummell, played by Robert Conrad.

12
00:38:08,571 --> 00:38:14,571
D.J., played by Martin Mull.

13
00:41:19,285 --> 00:41:25,285
Jingle All the Way is a 1996 American Christmas family
comedy film directed by Brian Levant.

14
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:36,000
The film stars Arnold Schwarzenegger and Sinbad as Howard Langston and Myron Larabee, two rival fathers living in Minneapolis desperately
trying to purchase the popular Turbo Man action figure for their respective sons on a last-minute shopping spree on Christmas Eve.

15
00:47:40,714 --> 00:47:46,714
The film's title is borrowed from the lyrics of the
popular Christmas song "Jingle Bells".

16
00:50:51,428 --> 00:50:57,428
The film was written by Randy Kornfield.

17
00:54:02,142 --> 00:54:08,142
Producer Chris Columbus rewrote the script, adding in elements of satire about the
commercialization of Christmas, and the project was picked up by 20th Century Fox.

18
00:57:12,857 --> 00:57:18,857
Delays to Fox's reboot of Planet of the Apes allowed Schwarzenegger to come on board the
film, while Columbus opted to cast Sinbad instead of Joe Pesci as Myron.

19
01:00:23,571 --> 01:00:29,571
Jingle All the Way was set and filmed in the Twin Cities of Minneapolis and Saint
Paul at a variety of locations, including the Mall of America.

20
01:03:34,285 --> 01:03:40,285
After five weeks of filming, production moved to California,
where scenes such as the end parade were shot.

21
01:06:45,000 --> 01:06:51,000
The film's swift production meant merchandising was limited to a
replica of the Turbo Man action figure used in the film.

22
01:09:55,714 --> 01:10:01,714
Upon release, Jingle All the Way grossed $129.8 million but received
generally negative reviews from critics for its uneven tone.

23
01:13:06,428 --> 01:13:12,428
In 2001, Fox was ordered to pay $19 million to Murray Hill Publishing for stealing
the idea for the film; the verdict was overturned three years later.

24
01:16:17,142 --> 01:16:23,142
Jingle All the Way is the third and final collaboration between Sinbad and Phil Hartman after Coneheads (1993) and Houseguest
(1995), and the last film featuring Hartman to be released during his lifetime before he was murdered in 1998.

25
01:19:27,857 --> 01:19:33,857
In 2014, the film was followed by a sequel in name only, Jingle
All the Way 2, starring Larry the Cable Guy.

26
01:22:38,571 --> 01:22:44,571
The film explores themes of husband wife relationship,
holiday, minnesota, christmas party, santa claus.

27
01:25:49,285 --> 01:25:55,285
The tone is lighthearted.
